\section{Coordinate order}{

The column order of \code{obsm/spatial} is not fixed by the AnnData spec and the
two academic collections this reader was built against were checked file by
file rather than assumed. It gets resolved in this order:

\enumerate{
\item Whether the spots land on the tissue in the image the file ships.
\item Whether only one order fits inside that image at all.
\item The \code{obs} \code{pxl_row_in_fullres} / \code{pxl_col_in_fullres} labels. Last,
because \code{scanpy.read_visium} swaps those two names relative to Space
Ranger, so they mean the opposite in any file that went through it.
\item \code{assume_orientation} out of \code{\link[=params_sp_h5ad_io]{params_sp_h5ad_io()}}.
}

What was used is reported at load time and a warning is raised when nothing
in the file settled it. Note that a swap of \code{x} and \code{y} is a reflection and
preserves every pairwise distance, so the graph, Moran's I, SPARK-X and
neighbourhood enrichment are all numerically identical either way. The one
thing that cares is \code{\link[=image_features_sp]{image_features_sp()}}.
}

\section{Graph layouts}{

\code{"knn"} and \code{"radius"} always work. \code{"hex"} and \code{"square"} are exact
look-ups over \code{array_row}/\code{array_col} and need those columns in \code{obs}, which
\code{obsm/spatial} does not supply. The reader warns at load time when they are
missing rather than letting \code{\link[=build_spatial_graph_sp]{build_spatial_graph_sp()}} fail later.
}

\section{One sample per object}{

Like \code{\link[=load_visium]{load_visium()}}, this rewrites the whole store, so it errors
when the object already carries a different sample. Re-running with the same
\code{exp_id} is fine.
}

